  "message": "fix(acot, acoth): do not extract a minus sign at a possible branch point (#30590)\n\nFixes #30588.\n\n`acot.eval` and `acoth.eval` both rewrite `f(-z)` to `-f(z)` whenever\nthe\nargument can have its minus sign extracted. That identity holds\neverywhere\nexcept at `z = 0`, which is exactly where both functions are\ndiscontinuous:\n\n* `acot(0) = pi/2`, but `-acot(0) = -pi/2`\n* `acoth(0) = I*pi/2`, but `-acoth(0) = -I*pi/2`\n\nSo the rewrite bakes the wrong value into the expression for any later\nsubstitution at the branch point:\n\n```pycon\n>>> from sympy import acot, acoth, symbols\n>>> x = symbols('x')\n>>> acot(-x)\n-acot(x)\n>>> acot(-x).subs(x, 0), acot(0)\n(-pi/2, pi/2)\n>>> acoth(-x).subs(x, 0), acoth(0)\n(-I*pi/2, I*pi/2)\n```\n\n## Fix\n\nRestrict the rewrite to arguments that are known to be nonzero, by\nrequiring a known-nonzero argument, with the cheaper\n`arg.is_nonzero` check first:\n\n```python\nif arg.is_nonzero and arg.could_extract_minus_sign():\n    return -cls(-arg)\n```\n\nBoth call sites are guarded this way, so a symbol that may vanish at 0\nkeeps\nits original form and a substitution now agrees with direct evaluation:\n\n```pycon\n>>> acot(-x).subs(x, 0), acot(0)\n(pi/2, pi/2)\n>>> acoth(-x).subs(x, 0), acoth(0)\n(I*pi/2, I*pi/2)\n```\n\nEverything that is provably nonzero still rewrites exactly as before:\n\n```pycon\n>>> n, p = symbols('n', negative=True), symbols('p', positive=True)\n>>> acot(-n), acot(-p), acot(-1), acot(-sqrt(2))\n(-acot(n), -acot(p), -pi/4, -acot(sqrt(2)))\n```\n\n## Scope\n\nThis is deliberately limited to `acot` and `acoth`. `atan` has the same\n`could_extract_minus_sign()` branch, but `atan` is analytic at 0, so\n`atan(-z) == -atan(z)` is valid there and `atan` is deliberately left\nalone.\n\nThe other odd inverse trigonometric functions are unaffected for the\nsame\nreason: `sin`, `tan`, `cot` and `asin` are continuous at 0. `acot` is\nthe\nonly strictly-odd inverse trig function with a branch point at the\norigin, and\n`acoth` is its hyperbolic counterpart.\n\n## Tests\n\nTwo existing assertions pinned the old behaviour and are updated, since\nthe\nbehaviour they pinned is the bug:\n\n* `test_acot`: adds coverage for the nonzero-argument rewrite, for the\nno-rewrite case, and for substitution at the branch point and away from\nit.\n* `test_evenodd_rewrite`: `acot` is removed from the strictly-odd list,\nwith a\n  comment explaining that it is odd only away from its branch point. The\nnumeric members of `with_minus` and a provably nonzero argument are\nstill\n  asserted to rewrite.\n* `test_acoth`: replaces `acoth(-x) == -acoth(x)` with the guarded\nbehaviour.\n\nRun locally:\n\n* `bin/test sympy/functions/elementary/tests/test_trigonometric.py\nsympy/functions/elementary/tests/test_hyperbolic.py` \u2014 168 passed\n* `bin/test sympy/functions/ sympy/simplify/ sympy/solvers/\nsympy/integrals/ sympy/core/` \u2014 3861 passed\n* `bin/test sympy/series/ sympy/assumptions/ sympy/calculus/\nsympy/utilities/tests/test_pickling.py` \u2014 722 passed\n* `bin/doctest sympy/functions/elementary/{trigonometric,hyperbolic}.py`\n\u2014 29 passed\n* `bin/test quality` \u2014 6 passed\n\nNo failures in any of the above.\n\n\n#### AI Generation Disclosure\n\nThis pull request was developed with AI assistance (OpenCode / LLM\ntooling).\n\n- The bug analysis for `#30588`, the `arg.is_nonzero` guard in\n`acot.eval` / `acoth.eval`, the corresponding test updates in\n`test_trigonometric.py` / `test_hyperbolic.py`, and the PR description /\nrelease-notes wording were produced with AI assistance and then reviewed\nand run locally by me.\n- Follow-up on reviewer request: `.mailmap` entry for `Yi-111-a`, and\nreordering to `arg.is_nonzero and arg.could_extract_minus_sign()`\n(cheaper check first), also AI-assisted and verified with\n`bin/mailmap_check.py` and the affected tests.\n\nI understand and can explain the change: the oddness rewrite is invalid\nat the branch point `z = 0`, so it must only fire when the argument is\nknown nonzero.\n\n<!-- BEGIN RELEASE NOTES -->\n* functions\n* Fixed `acot(-x)` and `acoth(-x)` being rewritten to `-acot(x)` and\n`-acoth(x)`, which gave an incorrect result after substituting `x = 0`.\n<!-- END RELEASE NOTES -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Yi-111-a <41800311+Yi-111-a@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"
 },
