  "message": "printing: elementwise function application notation for array expressions (#30572)\n\n<!-- DO NOT DELETE OR REPLACE THIS TEMPLATE or the PR will be closed.\n\nRead our Policy on AI Generated Code and Communication at\nhttps://docs.sympy.org/dev/contributing/ai-generated-code-policy.html.\n\nAs required in the policy do not use AI-generated text to complete the\nPR\ndescription below or the PR will be closed. Follow the instructions in\nthe\ntemplate below and keep all section headings or the PR will be closed.\n\nThe PR title above should be a short description of what was changed. Do\nnot\ninclude the issue number in the title. -->\n\n#### References to other Issues or PRs\n\nFollows 2056b30d01, which already prints ArrayTensorProduct with\nboxtimes.\n\n#### Brief description of what is fixed or changed\n\nPrint elementwise function application as in the matrix derivatives\npage, so `exp_o(A)` instead of `(d -> exp(d))_o(A)`.\nThis now also works for ArrayElementwiseApplyFunc, which had no LaTeX or\npretty printer before.\n\n#### Other comments\n\n`exp` now prints as `\\exp` in LaTeX when printed alone.\n\n(This description was written by an AI agent, Claude Code.)\n\n#### AI Generation Disclosure\n\nClaude Code (Claude Opus 5.5) generated the whole commit: changes in\n`sympy/printing/conventions.py`, `sympy/printing/latex.py`,\n`sympy/printing/pretty/pretty.py` and the tests in `test_latex.py` and\n`test_pretty.py`.\n\n#### Release Notes\n\n<!-- BEGIN RELEASE NOTES -->\n* printing\n* Elementwise function application of matrix and array expressions is\nprinted as `f` with a ring subscript, e.g. `exp_o(A)`, instead of a\nlambda.\n<!-- END RELEASE NOTES -->\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"
 },
